A||||21||Soelas||Modifying the doors||||1||
Z||000000||Soelas||07-17-2001||12:47 AM||||Well, slowly but surely, I'm on my way to installing a v8 into my Fiero. Right now $$$ is a major obstacle so I'm working on some of the other mods I had in mind. <P>I was going to convert the doors to suicide, but quickly realized that I'd have to lose about 100lbs to get in and out of the car once it's done.....<P>So, I've decided to modify the doors so they open similar to a lamborghini. Anyone know of any cars that are done like this? If there's a product I could buy?<P>I'd like to know before I go to a hell of a lot of work to engineer this. (besides buying a scrap fiero to do all the prototype work on).||||reg||1
Z||000001||Songman||07-17-2001||01:27 AM||||There has been a lot of talk on the forum about a project such as that... but as far as I know no one has ever done it. The consensus seems to be that you would have to just about totally re-engineer the car AND reshape the doors. Do a search and see what you can come up with... <P>Maybe search the Tech or General sections for Lambo doors or something like that...<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 07-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||gabriel||07-17-2001||10:39 AM||||Greetings:<P>&gt;. Anyone know of any cars that are done like this? If there's a product I could buy?<P>  Okay ... The main problems are that you will have to convert the Fiero's frameless window to a framed window (i.e. fabricate it yourself) - then modify the roof subframe to accommodate that, along with the roof skin and weatherstripping, not to mention the hinge and latch mechanics.<P>   It's a large job to tackle ... That being said, it's being done with various Lambo clone kitcars. - Check the sites for the different kitcar manufacturer for more detail, but after that, you are pretty much on your own.  I know of no conversion kit for such project.  If you find one, let me know.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>  &gt;besides buying a scrap fiero to do all the prototype work on).<P>   Seems like that is the path you are headed to.  Good luck. -g||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steve Normington||07-17-2001||11:02 AM||||I think that Lamborgini doors pivot from the front, not from the roof. If this is the case, then there would be no need to modify the roof/window frame.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||07-17-2001||12:30 PM||||The Lambo kits have a totally different body. the doors with the hinges are mounted in the front fender section rather than the stock door jam/post. with all the modifying that would have to be done, it would be cheaper in the long run to buy a lambo kit to start with.||||reg||1
Z||000005||b lo 0||07-17-2001||12:40 PM||||Lambo doors do pivot from the front, but you still would have to frame the window in order to get the door seal at an angle which would seal when the door is closed without breaking the window or having to roll the window down to open or close the door.  Not a flame, just MHO.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Soelas||07-17-2001||11:08 PM||||Hehe.... I like the comments already. I think the key word here is "like" or "similar". Meaning...."close, but no cigar".<BR>I'm not building a lamborghini. I'm modifying a Fiero. I own a Fiero, I've got the doors off and apart in a zillion pieces. I know the challenge that lies ahead of me.<BR>I just wanted to know if someone had already done what I'm about to try to do....I didn't want to know if YOU thought it was possible or not.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000007||killerb15||07-17-2001||11:29 PM||||Lambo doors are nice, but I have always dreamt of creating gullwing doors, like off of a Delorean. I think this would probably be easier than lambo-style doors, but there still is the issue of framing the glass on existing doors, and strengthening the roof of the car, wouldn't work too well on a t-roof car though. Best of luck with your V8 job!||||reg||10
Z||000008||Soelas||07-17-2001||11:36 PM||||Ok, just did some (15 minutes or so) research on Lamborghini kits. trying to see how the "kit car community" has handled the door problem and what the end result is.....<P>you know what was VERY disturbing?<P>The fact that I only found pictures of ONE specific Fiero/lamborghini conversion with the doors OPEN. The rest of the pics I saw of lamboghini kit cars ALL had the doors closed!!!<P>I wonder why this is?<BR>1. The manufacturer's don't wants guys like me seeing how they did it and build our own doors?<BR>2. The manufacturer's don't want people to see how crappy they actually work?<P>Regardless, I've got several ideas running in my head about converting the fiero doors to a "lambo-style". 2 of which use and electric motor and one of which uses a gas shock.<P>the gas shock idea looks like it's gonna be ruled out due to space confinements. If I actually pull it off?<BR>I PROMISE to show pics on my site.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||10
Z||000009||rogergarrison||07-18-2001||08:24 AM||||on the Lambo kits, the sides of the roof curve way in, lots more than Fiero, thats why you have to also cut the whole roof off when building the kit. You have to have the lambo roof. a stock Fiero the side windows are close to being vertical, any door that would be made to swing up from the front would not clear the windshield post for a starter. also it order to swing up the side glass would have to stick out beyond the roof to clear it, so there would be no way to seal it from the weather. So any way to do it without removing and building your own roof would require a hinge that slides out away from the car-then swing up. anything that complicated would require complicated engineering and machine work. If it did work im sure there would be thousands of dollars invested. <P>after thinking more on this, you may get away with something along the lines of the doors on the McLaren F1. they open up AND outward at roughly a 45* angle. It would still be a lot of work but i do think that that would be doable. If you dont know what they look like, let me know ill post a pic or 2.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 07-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Soelas||07-18-2001||07:40 PM||||So any way to do it without removing and building your own roof would require a hinge that slides out away from the car-then swing up. anything that complicated would require complicated engineering and machine work. If it did work im sure there would be thousands of dollars invested. <P>Finally...someone who's thinkin' out of the box  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>after thinking more on this, you may get away with something along the lines of the doors on the McLaren F1. they open up AND outward at roughly a 45* angle. It would still be a lot of work but i do think that that would be doable. <BR>I might be doing it that way...some sort of eccentric hinge.<P>If you dont know what they look like, let me know ill post a pic or 2.<P>Would love to see pics of them.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||07-18-2001||07:58 PM||||heres a top, side, front view. is hinged with one at top of A-pillar and one at Bottom of A-pillar<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P1000440.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||07-18-2001||08:00 PM||||An item that might help....<P>Find a car that has the type of doors you are after at a car show and photograph it from diferant angles with the door open.<P>Find the shop book for the car in question. A hinge as complcated as these has to be in there. There are very likely special tools and prcedures to adjust it. That sort of data usually has pictures and drawings.||||reg||1
Z||000013||rogergarrison||07-18-2001||08:04 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P1000439_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||07-18-2001||08:07 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P1000438_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||07-18-2001||08:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by killerb15:<BR><B>Lambo doors are nice, but I have always dreamt of creating gullwing doors, like off of a Delorean. I think this would probably be easier than lambo-style doors, but there still is the issue of framing the glass on existing doors, and strengthening the roof of the car, wouldn't work too well on a t-roof car though. Best of luck with your V8 job!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>actually killer if you did the gullwing (DeLorean) type, a T-top would be the ideal one. use the T-bar for the hinge support. You would have to use side sections of a coupe roof to build a frame around the side window similar to a Ford GT40 where the top of the door cuts almost to the center of the roof.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||b lo 0||07-19-2001||01:35 AM||||OOOH OOOH!  Damn, I'm smart!  A bit of work, but cool NONETHELESS!  Use a large diameter pin (about 2-3 inches thick) and put it in the front of the door opening perpendicular to direction of travel.  Put a key slot in the pin, key the door onto the pin, and inside the body of the car, key in an arm attached to a pneumatic strut (capable of rotating the entire door)between the back of the pin and the frame of the car.  Add another strut on the end of the pin, pushing from the center of the car.  Add a slide-joint ball bearing pivot where the pin exits the car body, and VOILA!  No framing the window, and when you open the door it first moves out AWAY from the car 3-4 inches, THEN scissors up into the open position!  Stock look until you open the door, and can even use the stock latches!  Catch my drift?  I'll post some drawings if you want.||||reg||1
Z||000017||GodSend||07-19-2001||02:45 AM||||You totally lost me (and im sure alot of other people)  Some pics would be great.<P>ON a side note, to anyone attempting this.  If you do is successfully, consider me inline to purchase one of your kits.  As long as the price is not astronomical (ie, great then a couple hundred bucks per kit) then Im sure you could sell a few (something to keep in mind when looking at the cost of research, initial construction)||||reg||1
Z||000018||kinboyatuwo||07-19-2001||03:18 AM||||i have seen one and know the shop that did it, the hinge is the hard part and it is pricy<P>i can get the name of the shop and see if you can make a deal<P>i looked into it and it is a long term project<P>kinboyatuwo@yahoo.com<P>email and i will look into it<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/b4lower50.jpg"> <BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."<P>87 Duke, 235,000km||||reg||6
