Z||000021||Will||07-20-2001||03:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by b lo 0:<BR><B>OOOH OOOH!  Damn, I'm smart!  A bit of work, but cool NONETHELESS!  Use a large diameter pin (about 2-3 inches thick) and put it in the front of the door opening perpendicular to direction of travel.  Put a key slot in the pin, key the door onto the pin, and inside the body of the car, key in an arm attached to a pneumatic strut (capable of rotating the entire door)between the back of the pin and the frame of the car.  Add another strut on the end of the pin, pushing from the center of the car.  Add a slide-joint ball bearing pivot where the pin exits the car body, and VOILA!  No framing the window, and when you open the door it first moves out AWAY from the car 3-4 inches, THEN scissors up into the open position!  Stock look until you open the door, and can even use the stock latches!  Catch my drift?  I'll post some drawings if you want.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>How about something like a Bendix drive (similar to what starters use). With that you just turn the support shaft. The first half turn pushes the door out, then when the drive reaches the end of it's linear travel it turns the door with it, and the last quarter turn opens the door.||||reg||1
